WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.262
Here's the catch.

2
00:00:03.262 --> 00:00:06.174
You have a camera, a compiled detector, and a frame in memory.

3
00:00:06.174 --> 00:00:09.086
Surely you can just run the model?

4
00:00:09.086 --> 00:00:11.998
Look at the boundary.

5
00:00:11.998 --> 00:00:14.910
The camera gave you nineteen twenty by ten eighty pixels.

6
00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.822
The model expects a particular tensor: different dimensions, perhaps a different colour order, and a different

7
00:00:17.822 --> 00:00:20.734
numerical scale.

8
00:00:20.734 --> 00:00:23.646
A pointer doesn't remove that disagreement.

9
00:00:23.646 --> 00:00:26.558
Something has to transform the image into the model's input.

10
00:00:26.558 --> 00:00:29.470
That surrounding vision work is where VART-X starts to earn its place.

11
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:33.940
First, fit the image to the required geometry.

12
00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:38.230
Letterboxing preserves the aspect ratio and fills the remaining space.

13
00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:42.520
Then arrange the channels and convert the values according to the model's contract.

14
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:46.810
Here, dividing a colour value by two hundred and fifty five is just an illustrative normalization, not a recipe for

15
00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:51.100
every model.

16
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.390
VART-X provides preprocessing modules for these operations, plus frame and memory abstractions for the buffers.

17
00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:59.680
Some processing can use programmable logic; some uses software.

18
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.970
It isn't all work for the neural accelerator.

19
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:07.537
Now the input is ready.

20
00:01:07.537 --> 00:01:10.923
What makes the model run?

21
00:01:10.923 --> 00:01:14.310
With VART-ML, the application creates a runner for the compiled model, queries its tensor requirements, supplies

22
00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.697
buffers, and calls execute.

23
00:01:17.697 --> 00:01:21.083
NpuTensor describes the data and its backing memory.

24
00:01:21.083 --> 00:01:24.470
The runner can also accept asynchronous work.

25
00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.857
So these names describe different jobs: VART-X prepares the vision data; VART-ML supplies the inference interface.

26
00:01:27.857 --> 00:01:31.243
And the output is still tensors.

27
00:01:31.243 --> 00:01:34.630
We haven't somehow acquired a photograph with rectangles already drawn on it.

28
00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.534
What if the application already uses ONNX Runtime?

29
00:01:38.534 --> 00:01:42.259
It can create a session, enable AMD's Vitis AI execution provider, and call Run.

30
00:01:42.259 --> 00:01:45.983
Supported graph work goes through the AMD provider; other supported work can execute on the CPU.

31
00:01:45.983 --> 00:01:49.708
That's the onnxruntime vitisai route.

32
00:01:49.708 --> 00:01:53.432
Notice the fork.

33
00:01:53.432 --> 00:01:57.157
You choose an application interface; you don't run ONNX Runtime and then invoke a Vart runner as a second inference

34
00:01:57.157 --> 00:02:00.881
stage.

35
00:02:00.881 --> 00:02:04.606
VART-X's vision operations can surround either route.

36
00:02:04.606 --> 00:02:08.330
A branch tells us something a list of package definitions doesn't.

37
00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:12.206
Now look inside the model.

38
00:02:12.206 --> 00:02:15.902
These are operators, not pixels.

39
00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:19.598
The VOE package supplies AMD's ONNX integration machinery, including provider libraries and graph partitioning,

40
00:02:19.598 --> 00:02:23.294
transformation and fusion passes.

41
00:02:23.294 --> 00:02:26.990
Grouping compatible work is a different operation from resizing the picture.

42
00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:30.686
Flex M L runtime supplies compiled-model loading and execution support: it reads metadata, checks the device, and

43
00:02:30.686 --> 00:02:34.382
invokes native execution with tensors.

44
00:02:34.382 --> 00:02:38.078
XRT provides lower-level device and buffer support.

45
00:02:38.078 --> 00:02:41.774
This is a functional map; the packages can contain overlapping support libraries.

46
00:02:41.774 --> 00:02:45.470
They aren't perfectly sealed layers.

47
00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:48.970
The inference call finishes.

48
00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:52.290
Here are scores and coordinates.

49
00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:55.610
To use them, a detector may need to decode candidates, filter them, and map coordinates back to the original image.

50
00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:58.930
Drawing comes after that.

51
00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.250
VART-X provides postprocessing and visualization components: PostProcess interprets outputs, MetaConvert prepares

52
00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:05.570
drawing metadata, and Overlay annotates the frame.

53
00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.890
Standard drawing runs on the CPU.

54
00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:12.210
Now the loop makes sense.

55
00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:15.530
Preparing an input, executing a model, interpreting its output and drawing a result are separate jobs.

56
00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:18.850
The names finally have somewhere to belong.

57
00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:23.290
That was the question: what do these components actually do?

58
00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:27.550
Follow the frame, and VART-X stops being a mysterious dependency.

59
00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:31.810
Follow the execution request, and VART-ML becomes an interface you could call.

60
00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:36.070
Follow the graph and compiled artifacts, and VOE and Flex M L have concrete work to do.

61
00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:40.330
This little explanation was written and rendered by an agent, with locally generated narration conditioned on one

62
00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:44.590
approved synthetic reference.

63
00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:48.850
The point isn't to replace building the application.

64
00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:53.110
It's to make the next encounter with its code less mysterious.
